    {
      "id": "form-ii",
      "title": "Configuring Connection (s)",
      "dimensions": "20 × 13 × 9\"",
      "medium": "etched Macbook lid and base",
      "year": 2025,
      "date": "2025-11-21",
      "image": "images/Trinity_Boy_Wharf_End_of_Year_2025-7893_1.png",
      "tags": [
        "sculpture",
        "selected"
      ],
      "description": "In ‘Configuring Connection(s)’, an erotic poem composed of cuttings from a Macbook instruction manual is etched into the body of a desktop Mac. The piece sees Macbooks as the ‘white cube’ of tech, bodies that attempt to visually neutralise themselves in the goal for the user to look through rather than at them. They aim to bring abstract digital space to the forefront, working to minimise their status as objects. In corroding the body of the Macbook, it is re-contextualised, foregrounding the physical, material reality of the technology. The piece translates the laptop into a language that exists outside of the mythic sanctity bestowed upon it by the deft marketing lingua used by Apple. Instead, both the language Apple uses (in the instruction manual), and the object of the laptop have been remorselessly de- and re-constructed. The corrosion of the aluminium spells out an erotic poem, illuminating the strange sensuality pervasive in the language of the instruction manual. The piece aims to uncover the physical and material bodies, both technological and human, that are obscured by the ostensibly asensorial digital. The object’s mutation throws up questions about the relationship between human and laptop, between metal and flesh, softening and complicating the sleek, unblemished and anti-human veneer of technology"
    },
